{"name":"Disc Jockey","hex":"#47c6ac","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"disc jockey","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who plays, and sometimes mixes, recorded music at nightclubs, dances, parties, or some other social event; or as a backup musician for spoken word, or hip hop performers.","A person who conducts a radio program of recorded music combined with talk, news, commercials, weather, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/disc_jockey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Disc jockey","blocks":[{"text":"A disc jockey, more commonly abbreviated as DJ, is a person who plays recorded music for an audience. Types of DJs include radio DJs, club DJs, mobile DJs, and turntablists. Originally, the \"disc\" in \"disc jockey\" referred to shellac and later vinyl records, but nowadays DJ is used as an all-encompassing term to also describe persons who mix music from other recording media such as cassettes, CDs or digital audio files on a CDJ, controller, or even a laptop. DJs may adopt the title \"DJ\" in front of their real names, adopted pseudonyms, or stage names."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Pete_Rock_%40_The_Brookyln_Bowl_in_2016.jpg/330px-Pete_Rock_%40_The_Brookyln_Bowl_in_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disc_jockey"},{"title":"Disc Jockey (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Disc Jockey is a 1951 American film directed by Will Jason and starring Ginny Simms, Tom Drake, Jane Nigh and Michael O'Shea. It was produced and distributed by Allied Artists."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a9/Disc_Jockey_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disc_Jockey_(film)"},{"title":"Jockey","blocks":[{"text":"A jockey is someone who rides horses in horse racing or steeplechase racing, primarily as a profession. The word also applies to camel riders in camel racing. The word \"jockey\" originated from England and was used to describe the individual who rode horses in racing. They must be light, typically around a weight of 100–120 lb. (45–55 kg), and physically fit. They are typically self-employed, and are paid a small fee from the horse trainer, whose colors they wear while competing in a race. They also receive a percentage of the horse's winnings. The job has a very high risk of debilitating or life-threatening injuries, not only from racing accidents but also, because of strict weight restrictions, from eating disorders."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Tony_dobbin.jpg/330px-Tony_dobbin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jockey"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Three_CDJs_and_two_SL-1210_at_Bob_Beaman_Nightclub_Munich.jpg/500px-Three_CDJs_and_two_SL-1210_at_Bob_Beaman_Nightclub_Munich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Three_CDJs_and_two_SL-1210_at_Bob_Beaman_Nightclub_Munich.jpg/1280px-Three_CDJs_and_two_SL-1210_at_Bob_Beaman_Nightclub_Munich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Three_CDJs_and_two_SL-1210_at_Bob_Beaman_Nightclub_Munich.jpg/500px-Three_CDJs_and_two_SL-1210_at_Bob_Beaman_Nightclub_Munich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"DJ booth in a nightclub, consisting of three CDJs (top), three turntables for vinyl records and a DJ mixer","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThree_CDJs_and_two_SL-1210_at_Bob_Beaman_Nightclub_Munich.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Robert_Hood_Live_%40_Kennedys%2C_Dublin%2C_Ireland_